Research Question
How do salary, unemployment rates, and state-level job demand vary across college majors?
More specifically, which college majors provide the strongest return on investment (ROI) based on salary and employment outcomes, and which states offer the greatest opportunities for careers associated with those majors?
The goal is to identify combinations of degree fields and states that provide favorable career prospects for graduates seeking strong employment opportunities and earning potential.
Cases
The cases will consist of college majors and their associated career outcomes.
Depending on the final datasets selected, there may be approximately 100 to 200 college majors and employment statistics across all 50 U.S. states.
Each observation will contain information about a major, salary levels, unemployment rates, and employment demand related to occupations associated with that major.
Data Collection
The data were collected through government surveys and labor market reporting programs.
Salary and unemployment information are expected to come from publicly available labor and educational datasets. State employment demand information will be obtained from labor market databases that track occupational employment and workforce demand across the United States.
No data will be collected directly from individuals for this project.
Type of Study

This is an observational study.
The project analyzes existing employment and education data without manipulating any variables or assigning treatments.
As a result, the analysis can identify associations between degree choice, salary, unemployment, and job demand, but it cannot establish cause-and-effect relationships.


